{
  "requirement": "Number of hexadecimal digits in the identity hash of an anonymous selector segment.\n\n```rust,ignore\npub const HASH_DIGITS: usize = 12;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.outlines.HASH_DIGITS` declares one public constant, `pub const HASH_DIGITS: usize = 12`. It is the number of hex digits in an anonymous selector segment's identity hash.\n\n- **Constant value.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors]` imports `HASH_DIGITS` from `outline::selector`. It asserts `assert_eq!(HASH_DIGITS, 12)`, which would fail if the value changed.\n- **Digit count in anonymous segments.** The same test checks every anonymous `line@…` selector in a plain-text outline:\n  - The prefix after `line@` has length `HASH_DIGITS`.\n  - Every character of the prefix is an ASCII hex digit.\n  - The prefix equals `structural_hash[..12]`.\n  - Identical lines share a selector, and different lines get different selectors.\n- **Public export.** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` checks that the constant is exported at the documented path. The requirement's `:test:` anchor names this test.\n\nThe requirement states nothing else. Parsing of hash segments, such as rejecting a wrong digit count, belongs to the selector-parser requirement, not this one. Markdown and code outlines use the same constant, but the requirement defines only the constant's value, which the test pins. I found no gap.",
